<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <RootNamespace>org.GraphDefined.Vanaheimr.XMPPConsole</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Two helpers of the chat log are checked directly: the escaping that keeps
      one event on one line, and the trimming of the punctuation a sentence
      leaves clinging to a URL. Both are decisions rather than plumbing - the
      first decides whether a log can be read by grep, the second whether a
      full stop becomes part of an address - and neither belongs in the public
      surface of a console application just to be testable.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="org.GraphDefined.Vanaheimr.XMPPConsole.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Only the log of the output any more: the console writes itself and needs
      a logger for that. BouncyCastle stood here as long as OMEMO lay here -
      since D97 it does not any more, and a package nobody uses is a claim
      about a dependency that does not exist.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging" Version="10.0.10" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Console" Version="10.0.9" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The pinned submodule first, a sibling checkout only if there is none.
      Two references, one of which is always off - not indecision, but the two
      trees this project is built in.

      Cloned on its own, one clone recursing into the submodules is the whole
      build: libs/Ratatoskr exists, pins a revision, reaches on for ..\..\Hermod
      and ..\..\Styx, which under libs/ are its siblings. That is what an
      application repository owes whoever clones it - not a restore error
      naming three paths outside the tree.

      Inside XMPPConformanceTests this is itself a submodule, and that checkout
      is deliberately not recursive: libs/XMPPConsole/libs/ stays empty, the
      condition below is false, and ..\..\Ratatoskr resolves to the
      libs/Ratatoskr *that* repository pins. Which is the whole point - it
      holds this console in its solution, and a recursive checkout would put a
      second Ratatoskr.csproj there, producing the same assembly twice.

      So the pin here never competes with the pin there. Delete the fallback
      and the conformance suite stops building; delete the submodules and a
      plain clone stops building.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\libs\Ratatoskr\Ratatoskr\Ratatoskr.csproj"
                      Condition="Exists('..\libs\Ratatoskr\Ratatoskr\Ratatoskr.csproj')" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Ratatoskr\Ratatoskr\Ratatoskr.csproj"
                      Condition="!Exists('..\libs\Ratatoskr\Ratatoskr\Ratatoskr.csproj')" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
